.TH VIM-ARCADE 1 "2026-08-04" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
vim-arcade \- play your live GitHub issue/PR queue as a vim\-arcade level
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vim-arcade
.br
.B vim-arcade \-\-map
.br
.B vim-arcade \-\-live
.br
.B vim-arcade \-\-map \-\-live
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vim-arcade
opens the open issues and pull requests of the repository you are standing
in as a vim\-arcade grid. Each item is a block on an otherwise open row;
ordinary vim motions walk to it; landing beside it opens the real title and
body with an action menu \(em comment, close, merge, mark a draft ready \(em
and each action shells out to the real
.BR gh (1).
Pressing
.B n
anywhere creates a new issue.
.PP
The target repository is resolved by
.B gh
from the
.I caller's
current directory, not from where
.B vim-arcade
is installed. That is what makes it a verb rather than a launcher: it is
correct from any repository on the machine, including ones that have no
checkout of vim\-arcade.
.PP
.B The default is a dry run.
Every action logs the command it would have run and runs nothing. Writing
requires
.BR \-\-live ,
spelled out.
.SH THE ENGINE TRAVELS WITH THE VERB
.B vim-arcade
is a shell front door over a stdlib\-only Python engine, built by CI into
one file,
.IR dist/vim\-arcade.pyz ,
and carried onto the same
.B bashified
branch as this page on every push to main
.RI ( .github/workflows/carry\-zipapp.yml ).
A standalone shallow clone of that branch, on a machine with no vim\-arcade
development checkout anywhere, is a complete
.BR vim-arcade .
.PP
This is deliberate and it is the difference between
.B vim-arcade
and its neighbour
.BR entraine (1),
which still reads
.B ENTRAINE_LEGACY_ROOT
and defaults to a path under
.IR ~/Documents/Projects .
The
.B vim-arcade
this page replaces was a hand\-installed symlink into a development clone,
owned by no installer and dead the moment that clone was removed.
.PP
.I ENGINE-PROVENANCE
records which main commit built the carried archive.
.B VIM_ARCADE_ENGINE_ROOT
overrides the pyz and points this verb at a live source checkout instead,
for a developer working on the engine itself. See
.I CONTRACT.md
for why a built artifact was chosen over a submodule, a subtree merge, or
carrying source (issue #131).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-map
Start zoomed OUT at the tile map of every repository across the accounts
.B vim-arcade
watches, rather than at the current repository. One open count and a
compact fresh/replied/draft summary per tile; no walkable grid and no item
actions at that zoom, because you cannot safely close something you cannot
read. Enter zooms into a tile;
.B M
returns to the map.
.B M
reaches the map from the single\-repo view too, so this flag is a starting
position and not a mode.
.IP
.B There is no separate joue\-panes verb, and this flag is why.
See HISTORY.
.TP
.B \-\-live
Perform the actions instead of logging them. Comments are posted, issues and
PRs are closed, merges are merged, new issues are created.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-dry\-run
State the default explicitly. Given together with
.BR \-\-live ,
this one wins and nothing is written: a command line that both authorises
and forbids writing is a caller who does not know which they meant, and
guessing "write" is the expensive guess.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Refused, always, with 7. See REFUSALS.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Accepted by the shared runtime and ignored:
.B vim-arcade
is a curses front end and has no non\-interactive output to shape.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
As above.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Usage summary. Works with no terminal, no
.BR gh ,
and no engine present \(em it is the one thing
.B vim-arcade
can always do.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version.
.SH COST
.B vim-arcade
cannot spend money. It carries no
.B \-\-summon
flag and no code path of it reaches a metered service. It does reach the
GitHub API through
.BR gh ,
which is not metered but
.I is
a real mutation of real state once
.B \-\-live
is given \(em which is why that flag, too, has no short form.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. An unknown flag, a positional argument, or
an abbreviated cost flag is this.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran and could not do the job. The engine is missing from this
checkout, or
.B python3
is not on PATH.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read its domain. No terminal to render into;
.B gh
not installed;
.B gh
not authenticated. The last of these matters most:
.B an unauthenticated gh still lists public repositories,
so the queue would come back short rather than empty, and a short queue that
looks healthy is indistinguishable from a quiet morning.
.TP
.B 7
REFUSED: out of scope on principle. See REFUSALS. No flag lifts it.
.PP
.B vim-arcade
never returns 3 or 4. It has no summon, and it has no action that is
contracted but unbuilt, so its
.B \-\-help
advertises only the codes above rather than the full vocabulary.
.SH REFUSALS
.TP
.B \-\-force will not override a refused merge
.B vim-arcade
re\-checks mergeability fresh before it will ever build a
.B gh pr merge
command, and refuses locally \(em naming the reason and the next action \(em
on a conflict, a draft, a blocked or failing check gate, or a PR whose
content is already superseded on its base. That last case is detected by
content overlap rather than by GitHub's own conflict flag, because a
superseded PR that still applies
.I cleanly
would otherwise merge silently.
.IP
None of those is lifted by a flag. A refusal that a flag can lift is a
warning wearing a refusal's clothes, and the one thing the merge guard
exists to prevent is a merge nobody looked at. The only second look
.B vim-arcade
offers is inside the detail panel, for the title/diff mismatch case alone,
where a warning naming the file count needs a second
.B m
press to proceed.
.TP
.B item actions at map zoom
No close, comment or merge key is dispatched from the tile map. A tile
carries a name and a count, not a body, and pressing
.B x
on something you have not read is the failure the map would otherwise make
cheap.
.TP
.B a second event loop
.B vim-arcade
has exactly one place any action key is dispatched. Zooming a tile rebuilds
the level for a different repository and falls through the same loop; it
does not spawn another. See HISTORY.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VIM_ARCADE_ENGINE_ROOT
Unset by default, which runs the carried
.IR dist/vim\-arcade.pyz .
Set it to a development checkout's root (a tree holding
.IR vim_arcade/ )
to run the verb against work in progress instead. It is an override and
not the default, because a default that reaches outside its own tree is
the shape this verb was created to retire.
.TP
.B GH_TOKEN, GH_HOST
Read by
.BR gh (1),
not by
.BR vim-arcade .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ENGINE-PROVENANCE
The source ref and sha of the main commit that built the carried
.IR dist/vim\-arcade.pyz .
Its presence is also the sentinel
.B vim_arcade.staleness.is_carried_engine()
uses to suppress the startup prompt's false "wrong branch" reading of a
carried checkout.
.TP
.I CONTRACT.md
The promise this utility must keep, obligation by obligation, and the
reasoning for carrying an engine on a branch whose README calls itself a
plain shell utility.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What the contract names and the tooling cannot yet do.
.SH EXAMPLES
Triage the repository you are standing in, changing nothing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ vim-arcade
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same, actually closing what you clear:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ vim-arcade \-\-live
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start at the cross\-repository map instead, from a directory that is not a
repository at all:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cd /tmp && vim-arcade \-\-map
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prove the verb introduces itself with no terminal, no engine and no
.B gh
\(em the one invocation that must always work:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ vim-arcade \-\-help | head \-1
vim-arcade \-\- play your live GitHub issue/PR queue as a vim\-arcade level
.fi
.RE
.PP
That last one is a doctest: its output is executed, not invented. The first
three are not, and this page says so rather than showing output it never
ran \(em all three open a curses session whose transcript is not text.
.SH HISTORY
Coined 2026\-08\-04, bringing under the bashify contract a script that had
existed since 2026\-08\-04 as
.I ~/.local/bin/joue
\(em a symlink into a development clone, installed by hand, owned by no
installer, and invisible to
.BR install-verbs.sh .
The same unowned\-symlink shape as the scheduler dispatch outage of
2026\-07\-29.
.PP
The name was briefly claimed twice: the
.B chezz
project also declared
.BR joue ,
and its
.B bashified
branch was retired on 2026\-08\-04 (tag
.IR retired-bashified )
because chezz is an end product rather than a development utility. The seam
the verb set is cut along is not shell versus Python; it is end products
versus the utilities you develop with. On that test a Python engine that
triages your live issue queue is squarely a verb.
.PP
Renamed from
.B joue
to
.B vim-arcade
on 2026\-08\-05. The command is a game, not a verb: the rest of this estate's
commands are things you tell the machine to do, and
.B joue
named the user's action rather than the thing being run. Zach, deciding it:
\(lqjoue should not even exist \(em vim arcade ships a game which should be
called something else, and that's what I should have on my path.\(rq The
same pass took
.BR entraine (1)
off the user path and removed the last dev clone of this project from
mandark.
.PP
.B joue\-panes
was a second front end with its own event loop. The two drifted the same day
both were touched \(em a merge guard existed in one and not the other \(em
and issue #39 collapsed them into one engine, leaving
.B joue\-panes
as a thin alias for
.BR "vim-arcade \-\-map" .
It is not carried here as a second verb. A verb owes a man page, and the
page for
.B joue\-panes
would be this page with one sentence changed, which is a second definition
of one thing and therefore the same drift in slower motion.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR entraine (1),
.BR gh (1),
.BR vim (1)
